\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Coordinate translation and phase multiplication}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{On a site #{v} of the coupled chain of \ref{fdip-00GV}, take
#{r=\eta e_v}, #{z=\ell e_v}, with #{\eta,\ell\in\mathbb R}.
The [Weyl operator](fdip-00H8) acts by
##{(W(\eta e_v,\ell e_v)f)(q)
 =\ue^{\ui \eta(q_v+\ell /2)}f(q+\ell e_v).}
It shifts one coordinate and multiplies by a phase. Its squared
norm on #{f} is #{\int|f(q+\ell e_v)|^2\,dq=\|f\|^2},
by translation invariance of Lebesgue measure.}

\p{Setting one parameter to zero isolates the two operations.
Applying their defining actions in opposite orders gives
##{\begin{aligned}
(W(\eta e_v,0)W(0,\ell e_v)f)(q)
 &=\ue^{\ui \eta q_v}f(q+\ell e_v),\\
(W(0,\ell e_v)W(\eta e_v,0)f)(q)
 &=\ue^{\ui \eta(q_v+\ell)}f(q+\ell e_v).
\end{aligned}}
Comparing these phases with the joint action yields
##{\begin{aligned}
W(\eta e_v,0)W(0,\ell e_v)
 &=\ue^{-\ui \eta\ell /2}W(\eta e_v,\ell e_v),\\
W(0,\ell e_v)W(\eta e_v,0)
 &=\ue^{\ui \eta\ell /2}W(\eta e_v,\ell e_v).
\end{aligned}}
Thus reversing phase multiplication and translation changes the
scalar factor by #{\ue^{\ui \eta\ell}}. This is the one-site instance
of the general Weyl multiplication rule in \ref{fdip-00HE}.}
